@.str.44 = private unnamed_addr constant [16 x i8] c"<scratch space>\00", align 1
@.str.45 = private unnamed_addr constant [688 x i8] c"More fine grained information about the stack layout is available by adding the\0A`-Rpass-analysis=stack-frame-layout` command-line flag to the compiler\0Ainvocation.\0A\0AThe diagnostic information can be saved to a file in a machine readable format,\0Alike YAML by adding the `-foptimization-record-file=<file>` command-line flag.\0A\0AResults can be filtered by function name by passing\0A`-mllvm -filter-print-funcs=foo`, where `foo` is the target function's name.\0A\0A   .. code-block:: console\0A\0A      clang -c a.cpp -Rpass-analysis=stack-frame-layout -mllvm -filter-print-funcs=foo\0A\0A   .. code-block:: console\0A\0A      clang -c a.cpp -Rpass-analysis=stack-frame-layout -foptimization-record-file=<file>\00", align 1
@.str.46 = private unnamed_addr constant [81 x i8] c"Emit a warning for each variable declaration that generates code run at startup.\00", align 1
@.str.47 = private unnamed_addr constant [912 x i8] c"The warning is issued if the number of pre-processor tokens exceeds\0Athe token limit, which can be set in three ways:\0A\0A1. As a limit at a specific point in a file, using the ``clang max_tokens_here``\0A   pragma:\0A\0A   .. code-block:: c++\0A\0A      #pragma clang max_tokens_here 1234\0A\0A2. As a per-translation unit limit, using the ``-fmax-tokens=`` command-line\0A   flag:\0A\0A   .. code-block:: console\0A\0A      clang -c a.cpp -fmax-tokens=1234\0A\0A3. As a per-translation unit limit using the ``clang max_tokens_total`` pragma,\0A   which works like and overrides the ``-fmax-tokens=`` flag:\0A\0A   .. code-block:: c++\0A\0A      #pragma clang max_tokens_total 1234\0A\0AThese limits can be helpful in limiting code growth through included files.\0A\0ASetting a token limit of zero means no limit.\0A\0ANote that the warning is disabled by default, so -Wmax-tokens must be used\0Ain addition with the pragmas or -fmax-tokens flag to get any warnings.\00", align 1
@.str.48 = private unnamed_addr constant [75 x i8] c"Warn about designated initializers with some fields missing (only in C++).\00", align 1
@.str.49 = private unnamed_addr constant [414 x i8] c"This warning is enabled when source code using the macros ``INFINITY`` or ``NAN``\0Ais compiled with floating-point options preventing these two values. This can\0Alead to undefined behavior. Check the order of command line arguments that modify\0Athis behavior, such as ``-ffast-math``, ``-fhonor-infinities``, and\0A``-fhonor-nans`` (etc), as well as ``#pragma`` directives if this diagnostic is\0Agenerated unexpectedly.\00", align 1
@.str.50 = private unnamed_addr constant [1273 x i8] c"Warns when objects which are supposed to be globally unique might get duplicated\0Awhen built into a shared library.\0A\0AIf an object with hidden visibility is built into a shared library, each instance\0Aof the library will get its own copy. This can cause very subtle bugs if there was\0Aonly supposed to be one copy of the object in question: singletons aren't single,\0Achanges to one object won't affect the others, the object's initializer will run\0Aonce per copy, etc.\0A\0ASpecifically, this warning fires when it detects an object which:\0A  1. Appears in a header file (so it might get compiled into multiple libaries), and\0A  2. Has external linkage (otherwise it's supposed to be duplicated), and\0A  3. Has hidden visibility.\0A\0AAs well as one of the following:\0A  1. The object is mutable, or\0A  2. The object's initializer definitely has side effects.\0A\0AThe warning is best resolved by making the object ``const`` (if possible), or by explicitly\0Agiving the object non-hidden visibility, e.g. using ``__attribute((visibility(\22default\22)))``.\0ANote that all levels of a pointer variable must be constant; ``const int*`` will\0Atrigger the warning because the pointer itself is mutable.\0A\0AThis warning is currently disabled on Windows since it uses import/export rules\0Ainstead of visibility.\00", align 1
@.str.51 = private unnamed_addr constant [259 x i8] c"**Note:** enabling this warning in C will change the semantic behavior of the\0Aprogram by treating all string literals as having type ``const char *``\0Ainstead of ``char *``. This can cause unexpected behaviors with type-sensitive\0Aconstructs like ``_Generic``.\00", align 1
